[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two behaviours of the C that the tests had to be written *around*, because the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first drafts asserted the wrong thing:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`cp_stored` copies from the wrong offset at most `in` alignments.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `cp_ptr` computes the memcpy source as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `(char *)(s->words + s->word_index) - (s->count / 8)`, which only lands on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the payload when `in` is 2 bytes past a 4-byte boundary — i.e. exactly what
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `load_png_mem` passes (`data + 2` off a 16-aligned `malloc`). Calling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `cp_inflate` directly with a 4-aligned buffer makes a stored block copy the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    LEN/NLEN bytes instead of the data. Row 1 therefore model-checks only the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `in_shift == 2` case and compares the other three alignments
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    differentially. The Rust reproduces all four.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **The pixel buffer's tail is uninitialised by design.** `img.pix` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `malloc((img.w+1)*img.h*4)` but `cp_convert` only writes `img.w*img.h*4`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    bytes, so the last `img.h*4` bytes are whatever `malloc` returned. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    harness therefore compares exactly the `w*h*4` bytes the C defines, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    forks the two children back-to-back from an identical heap so that even the
